    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186124471,
      "id": "ea1e5a55-a957-52cd-b693-0e48770672a6",
      "title": "Computing the Rate of Change of a Multivariable Function at a Point Using the Chain Rule"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "29c44b46-01ee-5c5c-ac1a-f39f70c37110",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=2 x z^{2} - 3 y^{3}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨3 t,-2,\\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}⟩$ at the point $t=2.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x z^{2} - 3 y^{3}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x z^{2} - 3 y^{3}),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2 x z^{2} - 3 y^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 z^{2},- 9 y^{2},4 x z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=3 t$, $y=-2$, $z=\\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}$ into the above to find $∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =⟨2 \\left(\\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}\\right)^{2},- 9 \\left(-2\\right)^{2},4 \\cdot 3 t \\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 t^{2} - 6,-36,12 t \\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3 t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t^{2} - 3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,0,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2 t^{2} - 6,-36,12 t \\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}⟩\\cdot ⟨3,0,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\left(2 t^{2} - 6\\right)\\cdot \\left(3\\right)+\\left(-36\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)+\\left(12 t \\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}}\\right) \\\\  & =6 t^{2} - 18+0+12 t^{2} \\\\  & =18 t^{2} - 18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(2))] & =18 (2)^{2} - 18 \\\\  & =6+0+48 \\\\  & =54.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
