    "question": {
      ":question/id": "bb27944b-a1d6-5dc9-8ea3-1288d51dcf29",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=x z^{2} - 3 y^{3}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,-3,\\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}⟩$ at the point $t=2.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x z^{2} - 3 y^{3}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x z^{2} - 3 y^{3}),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x z^{2} - 3 y^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨z^{2},- 9 y^{2},2 x z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t$, $y=-3$, $z=\\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}$ into the above to find $∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =⟨\\left(\\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}\\right)^{2},- 9 \\left(-3\\right)^{2},2 t \\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t^{2} - 3,-81,2 t \\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t^{2} - 3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨t^{2} - 3,-81,2 t \\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\left(t^{2} - 3\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(-81\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)+\\left(2 t \\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{t^{2} - 3}}\\right) \\\\  & =t^{2} - 3+0+2 t^{2} \\\\  & =3 t^{2} - 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(2))] & =3 (2)^{2} - 3 \\\\  & =1+0+8 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
