    "question": {
      ":question/id": "262fbc1e-2e23-5217-8a33-cf6e6fdce875",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\ln{\\left(x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} \\right)}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨- t + 1,2,- 2 t⟩$ at the point $t=-2.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln{\\left(x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} \\right)}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\ln{\\left(x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} \\right)}),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\ln{\\left(x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} \\right)})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2 x}{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}},\\frac{2 y}{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}},\\frac{2 z}{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=- t + 1$, $y=2$, $z=- 2 t$ into the above to find $∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =⟨\\frac{2 \\left(- t + 1\\right)}{\\left(- 2 t\\right)^{2} + \\left(- t + 1\\right)^{2} + 2^{2}},2 \\cdot 2 \\frac{1}{\\left(- 2 t\\right)^{2} + \\left(- t + 1\\right)^{2} + 2^{2}},\\frac{2 \\left(- 2 t\\right)}{\\left(- 2 t\\right)^{2} + \\left(- t + 1\\right)^{2} + 2^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- \\frac{2 t}{5 t^{2} - 2 t + 5} + \\frac{2}{5 t^{2} - 2 t + 5},\\frac{4}{5 t^{2} - 2 t + 5},- \\frac{4 t}{5 t^{2} - 2 t + 5}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- t + 1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 2 t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨- \\frac{2 t}{5 t^{2} - 2 t + 5} + \\frac{2}{5 t^{2} - 2 t + 5},\\frac{4}{5 t^{2} - 2 t + 5},- \\frac{4 t}{5 t^{2} - 2 t + 5}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,0,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{2 t}{5 t^{2} - 2 t + 5} + \\frac{2}{5 t^{2} - 2 t + 5}\\right)\\cdot \\left(-1\\right)+\\left(\\frac{4}{5 t^{2} - 2 t + 5}\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{4 t}{5 t^{2} - 2 t + 5}\\right)\\cdot \\left(-2\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\left(t - 1\\right)}{5 t^{2} - 2 t + 5}+0+\\frac{8 t}{5 t^{2} - 2 t + 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\left(5 t - 1\\right)}{5 t^{2} - 2 t + 5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at $t=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(-2))] & =\\frac{2 \\left(5 (-2) - 1\\right)}{5 (-2)^{2} - 2 (-2) + 5} \\\\  & =- \\frac{6}{29}+0- \\frac{16}{29} \\\\  & =- \\frac{22}{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
